impression could not have been a nicer  person he's he's very shy he's very  self-deprecating he has a great sense of  humor but I think  what struck me most was that he just  lacks any sort of cynicism which is one  of the reasons I wanted to do the movie  is that I think in our world today we're  surrounded by mostly cynical images and  thoughts and and and this was a person  completely lacking in that and and that  that moved me and struck me especially  in the face of what had happened to him  you know the victim of his hate crime  and he was able to come out the other  side and channel all of that anger and  pain into something really beautiful and  kind and productive among the many  horrible things that happened to mark is  that he didn't have a lot of insurance  and he was actually sent out of the  hospital before he was well because  whatever coverage he had was going to go  away he didn't have access to therapy he  didn't have things beyond you know the  basic things that got him going again so  how did he figure out that this was  going to be something that could help  him heal I don't know I I don't know the  answer to that I don't know how he  figured it out or whether it was just  some sort of intuitive response that he  had that he needed to keep his mind  occupied he couldn't use his hands  anymore but he needed to use his  imagination and I think and in talking  to him one of the things he said is that  he works at it every day and it's been  years since since his attack but he  works at it he challenges his brain he  knows that it the brain is a very  resilient thing and can make connections  around the connections that were  destroyed so so he challenges himself  and and forces himself to work at it and  he did it within something that he  really loved to do and was able to not  only just in terms of his dexterity in  terms of his trying to keep his mind  agile it was also a way of channeling  that that anger well creating these  scenarios in which he was this heroic  powerful figure that could get some sort  of redemption could you talk a little  bit about the physical production about  how you put the film together at what  point Bob is doing what  the animated sequences how he's using  voice actors how you yourself are  behaving in those scenes and then where  does that come in sequence to the actual  live physical production we did all of  the live-action stuff first and then  halfway through production we all got  into very tight gray jumpsuits and got  into a big room that was all green with  a lot of commotion capture stage motion  capture stage and they all sensors  picking up every everything you did and  it's the creepiest thing ever  it's though it's such a strange  experience but it was fun at the same  time because you just got to use your  imagination as he put it to me it's it's  black box theater at its most bare-bones  no there's no set there's no right you  might have steps but you don't really  have any physical problems ya know so  it's all X's on the wall and you're kind  of imagining things going on around you  but it was fun  and editorial you might have you know  additional dialogue replacement you  might come back and change things is  there any way to change a motion capture  performance or is that something that  Bob does without you and you say I don't  remember doing that or acting that way  it's like yeah I did I changed it well  that is actually one thing I said could  you make me do something that I didn't  do and he said oh yeah that's terrifying  like the thought so if I didn't smile  wide enough in a shot you could just add  more smile he's like yeah I could do  that if I felt like it but I don't think  he he did that we we did go back in and  pepper it with a couple of other shots  later on but and technically I don't  know how they how they blend that and it  how they fold it in you know that's it's  weird because we shot all this stuff on  this motion capture stage and there's  the conventional monitors and people you  know the script supervisor all of those  people but then literally behind a  curtain were another 30 people on  computers who were manipulating the  avatars of like of our doll bodies so  you could go back and watch a playback  of a very rough animation of the doll  body in the doll world to kind of get  your geography what were the most  important parts about mark that you  wanted to represent as an actor what did  you want to capture  and were some parts of that more  difficult than others first and foremost  is kindness in his generosity and his  resilience he you know after all of this  the fact that he came out the other side  and it doesn't isn't isn't bitter you  know he's he's just he's exudes this  warmth but also a sense of humor  and it was very important that his love  of shoes was a part of the movie because  that speaks to why there was a hate  crime in the first place and I think no  one wanted to take that component away  because I think it's extremely important  and the you touched on it briefly his  the the way he really idealizes women  women are and he made this very clear  women are the closest people in his life  the person who found him left for dead  was a woman you know all of that's true  all the people who nurtured him brought  him back to health continued to help him  all people he trusts most in the world  his confidence he derives great great  deal of strength and power from women  and I think that was an important aspect  of who he is because he he identifies on  on such a deep level with women and has  such a love and respect and affinity for  them that that was an important aspect  what is he doing now is he still have  this Belgian town out in his backyard he  you know what the good news is he box it  up he doesn't need it anymore he doesn't  he told us it's in boxes behind his  trailer he has other things he has other  scenarios I told I told him a story  about the first Academy Awards that I  was asked to be a presenter so I you  know my wife and I were walking the red  carpet and I stepped on a stars dress  and she screamed at me like what you  literally like oh yeah and I like  shriveled up into a little ball I was  terrified  and I told mark who it was and then  about two weeks later he started sending  me photographs of that exact scenario  and it was me on the red carpet going  and it was his star with this face like  and it really photorealistic it was  perfect  so he it's like now he's moved on to  wait did you forward that to the actress  no I yet okay now yeah keep going though  no no but but he's kid there's such a  joy in him like he takes things like  that and then he turns them into he uses  his world he uses his imagination and I  got a call from him when that first  trailer came out and he left a very  cryptic message and said hi Steve it's  mark call me and I thought oh [ __ ] you  know he didn't like it he doesn't like  the way this looks he doesn't like and I  got nervous cuz that that's really all I  care about  well at the end of this is that you know  he feels good about what we did and and  I called him back immediately and he  said I saw the trailer I've watched it  12 times I've cried every time and he's  like I love it and that world likes like  I would have done that if I could have  done that like that's the door that you  had leading up into the thing and he was  so excited and just was so warm about it  and again that generosity and here's the  thing too and Bob talked to him and said  they're going to be liberties take and  it's not going to be exactly you know  what you did not exactly a lot of it but  and he completely understood because  he's an artist himself and he  understands that as an artist Bob  Zemeckis was going to expand this one  side of it and turn it into something  you know through his imagination and  kind of take the ball and run with a  little bit further Steve great to see  you thanks for bringing us your films

